WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.440
DFIR depends on telemetry.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:12.500
Endpoint logs can show process creation, service changes, file modifications, PowerShell activity, scheduled tasks, USB usage, and security tool events.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:22.480
Windows Event Logs are especially important in many enterprise environments because they can reveal logons, privilege use, account changes, policy changes, and system activity.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:30.140
Endpoint Detection and Response telemetry may add richer behavioral context, but it should still be interpreted against the broader timeline.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.200
Identity and authentication logs often tell the story of access.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:45.860
They can show successful and failed logins, multi-factor prompts, impossible travel patterns, new device registrations, token activity, conditional access outcomes, and administrative actions.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:53.840
In many modern incidents, identities are the path through the environment, so authentication records can be as important as endpoint artifacts.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.500
Network and infrastructure data provide another angle.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:07.320
Firewall logs, proxy logs, DNS logs, VPN records, packet captures, and NetFlow can show connections between systems and external destinations.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:14.580
Email records can reveal phishing delivery, forwarding rules, message access, attachment activity, and user interaction.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:22.960
Cloud audit logs can show changes to storage, compute, identity, secrets, applications, and administrative consoles.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:29.500
SIEM data can help correlate these sources, but the SIEM is only as good as what it receives and retains.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.640
Backups and snapshots may support recovery and forensic comparison.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.140
The practical lesson is to know your data sources before the incident.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.760
Understand what is logged, how long it is retained, who can access it, how to export it, and what blind spots exist.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.340
During an incident, the team should not be discovering for the first time that critical evidence was never collected.
